# Security Guidelines

> Load this when touching auth, user data, secrets, or third-party integrations.

## Threat Model

- Malicious or malformed input from users
- API key theft or abuse
- Unauthorized provisioning of credentials
- Dependency vulnerabilities
- Secrets committed to the repository
- Injection attacks (SQL, command, XSS)

## Controls

- All secrets live in environment variables or a credential manager — never hardcoded, never committed
- Run dependency audits regularly (`npm audit`, `composer audit`, `pip-audit`, etc.)
- Validate and sanitize all user-facing inputs
- Use parameterized queries or the ORM — no raw string interpolation in SQL
- Hash passwords with a strong, slow algorithm; never store plaintext
- Log security events; never log PII, passwords, or tokens
- Use CSP and sandboxed iframes when rendering untrusted content
- Scan for secrets in pre-commit hooks

## Forbidden Patterns

- Committing `.env` files or API keys
- Returning raw HTML from Markdown without sanitization
- Storing plaintext credentials in logs or the database
- Disabling security lint rules to make code pass
- Trusting data from the client without server-side validation
